#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133486 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133926 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74196b81-0e52-5d10-a099-ccd849d03a98" :knowledge-point/title "Circulation Over Space Curves"}] :question/id #uuid "4f12e12d-37b3-5c41-9284-bc369ccd4c0f" :question/math-academy-id "e-12421" :question/problem "Calculate the circulation $\\Gamma$ of the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y,z) = y \\,\\mathbf{i} -x \\,\\mathbf{j} + z\\,\\mathbf{k}$ along the closed curve $C$ given by $\\mathbf r(t) =  \\sin (\\pi t)\\,\\mathbf{i} + \\cos (\\pi t)\\, \\mathbf{j} + t(2-t)\\,\\mathbf{k}$ for $t \\in [0,2].$" :question/worked-solution "The circulation $\\Gamma$ measures the total work done by a force $\\mathbf F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\Gamma = \\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm{d} \\mathbf r\n= \\int_a^b \\mathbf F(r(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf r'(t) \\; \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx = \\sin (\\pi t), \\qquad\ny = \\cos (\\pi t), \\qquad\nz = t(2-t) = 2t-t^2.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =y\\,i-x\\,j+z\\,k \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j+(2t-{t}^{2})\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf r'(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t))\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t))\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t-{t}^{2})\\,k \\\\  & =\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j+2(1-t)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j+(2t-{t}^{2})\\,k\\,)\\cdot (\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j+2(1-t)\\,k) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\cdot \\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t))\\cdot (-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t))+(2t-{t}^{2})\\cdot 2(1-t) \\\\  & =\\pi {cos}^{2}⁡(\\pi t)+\\pi {sin}^{2}⁡(\\pi t)+2(2t-{t}^{2})(1-t) \\\\  & =\\pi ({cos}^{2}⁡(\\pi t)+{sin}^{2}⁡(\\pi t))+2(2t-3{t}^{2}+{t}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\pi +4t-6{t}^{2}+2{t}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\pi +4t-6{t}^{2}+2{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[\\pi t+2{t}^{2}-2{t}^{3}+\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{4}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =[2\\pi +8-16+8]-[0] \\\\  & =2\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133487 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133927 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8f0629bb-3a37-5ab8-8449-50789b295c7a" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Line Integral Using Stokes' Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "87c9cf21-797c-5893-a81b-580685331739" :question/math-academy-id "e-12422" :question/problem "Use Stokes' theorem to evaluate \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\oint\\limits_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf{F} = \\langle -xz, \\: -yz, \\: x+z^2 \\rangle,$ and $C$ is the intersection of the paraboloid $z=x^2+y^2$ and the plane $z=1.$ The curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above." :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}\n= \\iint\\limits_S \\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C$ representing the intersection of the paraboloid $z=x^2+y^2$ and the plane $z=1.$ One such surface would be the plane $S$ with the equation \n\n$$\nz = g(x,y) = 1.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1)=0 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into the expression for $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf{F}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F\\,{|}_{z=g(x,y)} & =⟨-xz,\\,-yz,\\,{z}^{2}+x⟩{|}_{z=1} \\\\  & =⟨{{-x}_{⏟}}_{P},\\,{{-y}_{⏟}}_{Q},\\,{{1+x}_{⏟}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-(-x)\\cdot 0-(-y)\\cdot 0+1+x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(1+x)\\,\\text{d}A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane.\n\nNow, note that the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane is the circle $x^2+y^2=1$ of radius $1$ centered at the origin. Therefore, using the change of variables to polar coordinates\n\n$$\nx = r \\cos\\theta, \\qquad\ny = r \\sin\\theta, \\qquad\n\\textrm{d}A = r \\, \\textrm{d}r \\textrm{d}\\theta,\n$$\n\n \n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(1+x)\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(1+r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }r+{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[r\\theta +{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ]}_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2\\pi r\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =\\pi [{r}^{2}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133488 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133928 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d65a039f-6022-547d-a852-c249da5bd8ab" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Surface Integral Using Stokes' Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "f3f9694a-14f8-54c3-be32-744c2dd51736" :question/math-academy-id "e-12435" :question/problem "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by the closed curve $C$ with positive orientation and parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(t) = (t^2-t^3)\\,\\mathbf{i} + (t^2-t) \\, \\mathbf{j} + (t^2-t)\\,\\mathbf{k}$ for $t \\in [0,1).$\nGiven that $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z) = \\mathbf{i} + x\\, \\mathbf{j} + y\\,\\mathbf{k},$ evaluate the integral\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}\n= \\iint\\limits_S \\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx = t^2-t^3, \\qquad\ny = t^2-t, \\qquad\nz = t^2-t.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =i+x\\,j+y\\,k \\\\  & =i+({t}^{2}-{t}^{3})\\,j+({t}^{2}-t)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf r'(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-{t}^{3})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-t)\\,k \\\\  & =(2t-3{t}^{2})\\,i+(2t-1)\\,j+(2t-1)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(i+({t}^{2}-{t}^{3})\\,j+({t}^{2}-t)\\,k)\\cdot ((2t-3{t}^{2})\\,i+(2t-1)\\,j+(2t-1)\\,k) \\\\  & =1\\cdot (2t-3{t}^{2})+({t}^{2}-{t}^{3})\\cdot (2t-1)+({t}^{2}-t)\\cdot (2t-1) \\\\  & =2t-3{t}^{2}+(-{t}^{3}+2{t}^{2}-t)\\cdot (2t-1) \\\\  & =2t-3{t}^{2}-2{t}^{4}+{t}^{3}+4{t}^{3}-2{t}^{2}-2{t}^{2}+t \\\\  & =3t-7{t}^{2}+5{t}^{3}-2{t}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(3t-7{t}^{2}+5{t}^{3}-2{t}^{4})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}-\\frac{7}{3}{t}^{3}+\\frac{5}{4}{t}^{4}-\\frac{2}{5}{t}^{5}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{7}{3}+\\frac{5}{4}-\\frac{2}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{60}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431188 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133926 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74196b81-0e52-5d10-a099-ccd849d03a98" :knowledge-point/title "Circulation Over Space Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "58c1fd16-ca29-5709-a267-219424eaaabc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431156} {:answer/id #uuid "8c03109d-b971-53ee-a86a-7e35ed350bb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431160} {:answer/id #uuid "adea9c1e-27a3-5208-9dd6-795f2fa57e6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431165} {:answer/id #uuid "b705117a-dca8-5892-b76c-7de8a8a5594d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431169} {:answer/id #uuid "d1fa4475-9d5e-511a-8566-852d47df5af6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431171}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b705117a-dca8-5892-b76c-7de8a8a5594d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431169} :answer-field/id #uuid "42e70451-3a35-5ee4-bde4-603fef9a0a12" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431178}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9f4551bd-d0a1-5978-99b9-17e8a12314a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-162228" :question/problem "Calculate the circulation $Γ$ of the vector field $F(x,y,z)=z\\,j-y\\,k$ along the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=({t}^{2}-t)\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi t)\\,j+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi t)\\,k$ for $t\\in [0,1)$" :question/worked-solution "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx={t}^{2}-t,\\,y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi t),\\,z=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi t).\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =z\\,j-y\\,k \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi t)\\,j-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi t)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi t))\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi t))\\,k \\\\  & =(2t-1)\\,i-2\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi t)\\,j+2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi t)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi t)\\,j-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi t)\\,k)\\cdot ((2t-1)\\,i-2\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi t)\\,j+2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi t)\\,k) \\\\  & =0\\cdot (2t-1)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi t)\\cdot (-2\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi t))+(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi t))\\cdot 2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi t) \\\\  & =-2\\pi {sin}^{2}⁡(2\\pi t)-2\\pi {cos}^{2}⁡(2\\pi t) \\\\  & =-2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-2\\pi )\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2\\pi [t]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-2\\pi (1-0) \\\\  & =-2\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431183 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133926 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74196b81-0e52-5d10-a099-ccd849d03a98" :knowledge-point/title "Circulation Over Space Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11019ac0-2397-5082-8da2-e2cda61c67f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431149} {:answer/id #uuid "2ca8c25f-0afe-549b-bd9e-0579f77dfd35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431152} {:answer/id #uuid "617b3f2e-e124-52d6-b054-729c57763e06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431157} {:answer/id #uuid "8493f5bf-0293-5a89-b43a-eae5ba48387d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431159} {:answer/id #uuid "e69380fc-03e3-562e-97ec-2bf770b22d2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431174}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2ca8c25f-0afe-549b-bd9e-0579f77dfd35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431152} :answer-field/id #uuid "e84d6437-9ec9-5b4b-a3b3-8f14a689f608" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431181}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1d416162-8b5b-53c3-aa15-9f678fcebfbf" :question/math-academy-id "q-162229" :question/problem "Calculate the circulation $Γ$ of the vector field $F(x,y,z)=-y\\,i+x\\,j$ along the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j+2\\,k$ for $t\\in [0,2).$" :question/worked-solution "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t),\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t),\\,z=2.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =-y\\,i+x\\,j \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t))\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t))\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2)\\,k \\\\  & =-2\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j+0\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j)\\cdot (-2\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j+0\\,k) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\cdot (-2\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t))+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\cdot \\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)+0\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =2\\pi {sin}^{2}⁡(\\pi t)+2\\pi {cos}^{2}⁡(\\pi t) \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}2\\pi \\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi [t]_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =2\\pi (2-0) \\\\  & =4\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431186 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133928 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d65a039f-6022-547d-a852-c249da5bd8ab" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Surface Integral Using Stokes' Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3927b4fa-c581-50e7-9636-601f0c7f2db1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431154} {:answer/id #uuid "a2c0ebdd-99e9-5a8d-ba80-6dbf22ca7b81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431163} {:answer/id #uuid "ad732c00-dd52-57e7-a26e-fd44e8ff888d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431164} {:answer/id #uuid "b206cf8d-5ac9-5e6e-abb0-0881fe9594e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431167} {:answer/id #uuid "e08a22bc-8ede-59c0-a3f4-3fa119eeb7a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431173}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b206cf8d-5ac9-5e6e-abb0-0881fe9594e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431167} :answer-field/id #uuid "db93ea83-d9f2-5f0b-8dff-03ea91428db7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431179}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "60b9502a-1750-5d1a-ac6a-f7d7a17af2a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-162314" :question/problem "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by the closed curve $C$ with positive orientation and parametrization $r(t)=(2{t}^{3}-2t)\\,i+(t+{t}^{2})\\,j+(-t-{t}^{2})\\,k$ for $t\\in [-1,0).$\nGiven that $F(x,y,z)=y\\,i+x\\,k,$ evaluate the integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=2{t}^{3}-2t,\\,y=t+{t}^{2},\\,z=-t-{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =y\\,i+x\\,k \\\\  & =(t+{t}^{2})\\,i+(2{t}^{3}-2t)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{3}-2t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+{t}^{2})\\,j-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-t-{t}^{2})\\,k \\\\  & =(6{t}^{2}-2)\\,i+(1+2t)\\,j+(-1-2t)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =((t+{t}^{2})\\,i+(2{t}^{3}-2t)\\,k)\\cdot ((6{t}^{2}-2)\\,i+(1+2t)\\,j+(-1-2t)\\,k) \\\\  & =(t+{t}^{2})\\cdot (6{t}^{2}-2)+0\\cdot (1+2t)+(2{t}^{3}-2t)\\cdot (-1-2t) \\\\  & =2{t}^{4}+4{t}^{3}+2{t}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}(2{t}^{4}+4{t}^{3}+2{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[\\frac{2{t}^{5}}{5}+{t}^{4}+\\frac{2{t}^{3}}{3}]}_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =0-(-\\frac{2}{5}+1-\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431187 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133927 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8f0629bb-3a37-5ab8-8449-50789b295c7a" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Line Integral Using Stokes' Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ce41f48-3cd0-5535-a296-5747f3b20333" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{1+x}\\,y({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431150} {:answer/id #uuid "3859679f-08da-5297-b35b-fcbd0703da61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{1+x}\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431153} {:answer/id #uuid "83932af9-e25e-5cce-b094-ca4fbc00e243" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,y({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{3}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431158} {:answer/id #uuid "aeb9b526-feac-52a5-810d-3cdfe908fcdf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431166} {:answer/id #uuid "da95a228-ca5c-59a2-aafd-0aafd2625911" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{1+x}\\,({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431172}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1ce41f48-3cd0-5535-a296-5747f3b20333" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{1+x}\\,y({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431150} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3c5fce6-36d3-5f8a-948a-98bad40e6ffb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431182}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9aea1d19-f86f-5208-99cb-c76a6a49c45e" :question/math-academy-id "q-162364" :question/problem "Let $C$ be the intersection of the half-cone $z=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ and the boundary of the infinite vertical column enclosed by the planes $y=1+x,$ $x=0,$ and $y=0.$ Given that the curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above and $\\text{curl}\\,F=⟨0,\\,-{z}^{3},\\,0⟩,$ the circulation of $F$ along $C$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C$ representing the intersection of the half-cone $z=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ and the boundary of our infinite vertical column. One such surface is our paraboloid $S$ with the equation \n\n$$\nz=g(x,y)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F\\,{|}_{z=g(x,y)} & =⟨0\\,-{z}^{3},\\,0⟩{|}_{z=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}} \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{3/2},\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{{0}_{⏟}}_{P},\\,{{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{3/2}}_{⏟}}_{Q},\\,{{0}_{⏟}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-0\\cdot \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}+({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{3/2}\\cdot \\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}+0)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](images/ab/ab155520a21d38483e8b0978a04972e1a96c8d273d23ca7aa36bb1846189b4c8.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}y({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{1+x}\\,y({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431184 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133927 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8f0629bb-3a37-5ab8-8449-50789b295c7a" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Line Integral Using Stokes' Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06f86a33-c575-56fe-bdb7-9730885ac9ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431147} {:answer/id #uuid "20c46104-aaee-5f81-87c8-e9c611ae6afd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431151} {:answer/id #uuid "54da2db0-1ce1-5f93-af27-c7663a8c8354" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431155} {:answer/id #uuid "c8614eac-4ce9-50b5-a743-5b04a0329efb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431170} {:answer/id #uuid "fe3f8a13-dd60-5d9d-81be-3575b6037de6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431176}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "20c46104-aaee-5f81-87c8-e9c611ae6afd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431151} :answer-field/id #uuid "257fc3b0-4eee-568e-ab48-ba72ee13cf19" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431177}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4f7d4767-ad54-585a-81f3-9a4a8f681379" :question/math-academy-id "q-162373" :question/problem "Use Stokes' theorem to evaluate \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\text{curl}\\,F=⟨x+y,\\,-x+y,\\,-2z⟩,$ and $C$ is the intersection of the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ and the paraboloid $z=(x-1)^{2}+(y+1)^{2}.$ The curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above." :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C$ representing the intersection of the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ and the paraboloid $z=(x-1)^{2}+(y+1)^{2}.$ One such surface would be the plane $S$ with the equation \n\n$$\nz=g(x,y)=(x-1)^{2}+(y+1)^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}((x-1)^{2}+(y+1)^{2})=2(x-1) \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}((x-1)^{2}+(y+1)^{2})=2(y+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into the expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F\\,{|}_{z=g(x,y)} & =⟨x+y,\\,-x+y,\\,-2z⟩{|}_{z=(x-1)^{2}+(y+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =⟨{{x+y}_{⏟}}_{P},\\,{{-x+y}_{⏟}}_{Q},\\,{{-2(x-1)^{2}-2(y+1)^{2}}_{⏟}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-(x+y)\\cdot 2(x-1)-(-x+y)\\cdot 2(y+1)-2((x-1)^{2}+(y+1)^{2}))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2((x+y)(1-x)+(x-y)(y+1)-(x-1)^{2}-(y+1)^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4(-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+2x-y-1)\\,\\text{d}A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane.\n\nNotice that the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ with radius $1$ centered at the origin. Therefore, using the change of variables to polar coordinates\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\text{d}A=r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\n \n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}4(-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+2x-y-1)\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }4(-{r}^{2}+2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1)\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(-{r}^{3}+2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -r)\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[-{r}^{3}\\theta +2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -r\\theta ]}_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-2\\pi {r}^{3}-2\\pi r)\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =-8\\pi {[-\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}-\\frac{{r}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-8\\pi (-\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-6\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431185 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133928 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d65a039f-6022-547d-a852-c249da5bd8ab" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Surface Integral Using Stokes' Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a5ee6b0-2be5-5581-a028-5db7075b5455" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431148} {:answer/id #uuid "9ceea66b-fcc6-5f70-83a1-36dc6fc02ef6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431161} {:answer/id #uuid "9df165e3-93dc-582f-b6c7-50fa8041ebef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431162} {:answer/id #uuid "b623ffee-6400-53b3-8d80-728480b3c9fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431168} {:answer/id #uuid "f29e0799-e667-5d4c-b0bf-49fdbd64159a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431175}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b623ffee-6400-53b3-8d80-728480b3c9fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431168} :answer-field/id #uuid "e70a93bf-0c70-586e-8362-c72402361cae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431180}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "54c148d3-85ed-598f-9fe2-1c4177e8c1c4" :question/math-academy-id "q-162999" :question/problem "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by the closed curve $C$ with positive orientation and parametrization $r(t)=({t}^{2}-t)\\,i+({t}^{2}-{t}^{3})\\,j+({t}^{2}-t)\\,k$ for $t\\in [0,1).$\nGiven that $F(x,y,z)=2z\\,i+3y\\,k,$ evaluate the integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx={t}^{2}-t,\\,y={t}^{2}-{t}^{3},\\,z={t}^{2}-t.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =2z\\,i+3y\\,k \\\\  & =(2{t}^{2}-2t)\\,i+(3{t}^{2}-3{t}^{3})\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-{t}^{3})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-t)\\,k \\\\  & =(2t-1)\\,i+(2t-3{t}^{2})\\,j+(2t-1)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =((2{t}^{2}-2t)\\,i+(3{t}^{2}-3{t}^{3})\\,k)\\cdot ((2t-1)\\,i+(2t-3{t}^{2})\\,j+(2t-1)\\,k) \\\\  & =(2{t}^{2}-2t)\\cdot (2t-1)+0\\cdot (2t-3{t}^{2})+(3{t}^{2}-3{t}^{3})\\cdot (2t-1) \\\\  & =(-3{t}^{3}+5{t}^{2}-2t)\\cdot (2t-1) \\\\  & =2t-9{t}^{2}+13{t}^{3}-6{t}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2t-9{t}^{2}+13{t}^{3}-6{t}^{4})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[{t}^{2}-3{t}^{3}+\\frac{13}{4}{t}^{4}-\\frac{6}{5}{t}^{5}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =1-3+\\frac{13}{4}-\\frac{6}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
